nt  income and expenses  using actuarial models companies can  value blocks of business to ensure  they're holding the correct level of  assets now and in the future to meet the  promises that they make to their  customers  also models can be used to set prices on  new products or to implement critical  risk management strategies  a key aspect of actual models is that  they're heavily dependent on assumptions  assumptions can really include anything  like economics management actions  expectations for future mortality rates  and customer behavior the latter of  which we're focusing on today  and it's extremely important to get uh  robust assumptions into our models  because if those assumptions are poor  we're going to have a big garbage in  garbage out problem and that's going to  lead to  unsuccessful business results  all right so here's one simple actual  model that we're going to play a little  bit with today the actuarial present  value  they don't all like they don't all look  like this this one just happens to apply  to a product that includes death  benefits  surrender benefits withdrawals and no  future premiums  the formula here just describes a  probability weighted present value of  future cash flows in each time step  we're calculating the probability of  survival  times expected claims times a discount  factor  and death benefits here occur with  probability q d surrenders occur occur  with probability q s and withdrawals are  only dependent upon survival  all right now let's move on to  experience studies  experience studies are what they sound  like they're just formal reports of  experience  some common variables and studies are  mortality rates surrender rates claim  incidence and benefit utilization rates  and we actually use experienced study  data to perform to inform decisions  about assumptions that go into our  models  and before we get into examples i need  to finally introduce the data set that  we're going to be using in these case  studies  this data set is based on simulated  experience for a deferred annuity  product  i'm using simulated data here because  real insurance data usually is some  combination of expensive or proprietary  or just too darn massive to include in a  20-minute talk  and uh also since this is simulated data  i just want to keep in mind here that  any  results shown in the following slides  are really just purely illustrative and  they're not meant to represent any  particular product or company's  experience  the data here that we're looking at is a  census of annuity policies some active  and some that terminated due to  surrenders  and i've split that data set into a  training and test set  the target variable in the data set is  called term which indicates whether or  not the customer surrendered their  contract so this is really going to be a  classification problem for us  however since the eventual goal of this  experience study and assumption setting  is to  create an assumption for an actuarial  model what we're really going to be  interested in  is  the probability of term being equal to  one  and also i just want to note that since  this is simulated data there is such a  thing as a correct surrender rate that  exists and we'll be seeing that in some  of the upcoming examples  uh some key features on this data set we  have i'll point out three the age of the  policy in years  uh two the issue age of the customer and  then three it's a field called inc gear  which we'll see a lot which is an  indicator as to whether the product was  sold with an optional guaranteed income  benefit  which is a common uh type of insurance  benefit on annuities that provides a  fixed amount of income for life even  after the annuities account balance  drains to nothing  all right and here's a typical exhibit  you might see in experience studies  this is just a crosstab that shows  experience by policy year  usually in these types of crosstabs it  will compare actual experience versus 