're going to look at two  predictive models we're going to use  we're going to use tidy models and we're  going to create a logistic and random  forest model  up first is our traditional approach  first we're going to build a quick  crosstab table using that exp stats  function from the prior section  and we're also going to group by policy  or an income guarantee  and that gives us an assumption like  this which looks just like the plot you  saw before  from here we could also decide to apply  rounding or smoothing or any other  adjustments we think  would help deal with non-intuitive data  one unavoidable question that pops up in  assumption setting all the time is how  do we handle unobserved time periods  that we need in our actuarial models and  this is where expertise and professional  judgment come into play  for this example we run out of  experience data after year 15 but our  model requires us to make some  assumption for what's going to happen  past that point so we got to figure out  what we're going to do and what we're  going to do for this example and this  isn't always the right answer but we're  just going to use a naive forecast which  is going to assume that future surrender  rates are going to be equal to the last  observed value  all right now let's move on to some  predictive models first we're going to  start with logistic regression  we're going to create a model spec  and then we're going to create a recipe  that allows us to capture some of those  non-linear patterns in surrender rates  and i'm also going to tack on some  useful interaction terms that i know are  going to work well for this problem  next we just combine this model spec  into a workflow and lastly we're going  to call fit  and here's how our model compared to  observed and correct experience  we're looking in this plot at the policy  year and the income guarantee variable  generally we have a pretty good fit here  except after year 12 or 13 the model  surrender rates decline a bit too fast  given the linear nature of this model  now let's look forward to the random  forest model  and it's like kind of highlights one of  the things i like about tidy models this  code is virtually identical to the  previous slide the only real difference  here is that i um added the random  forest function specified that i want to  use ranger instead of the glm function  i'm leaving pre-processing here almost  unchanged but i should also mention here  that typically this level of engineering  isn't necessary for  this type of model however since this  example doesn't include any tuning of  hyper parameters i wanted to just i  wanted to add some feature engineering  to ensure i'm going to get a decent fit  under the default configuration  in terms of performance no surprise here  this model looks even better than the  logistic  okay so we created all these assumption  models what's next  if this were a typical machine learning  framework we'd compare our performance  against a test data set using common  performance metrics like roc area under  the curve or mean lock mean log loss etc  but in this section we're going to do  something a bit non-traditional by  testing performance with an actuarial  present value model  i'm going to gloss over the coding  details in this section but here's the  general process we're following first  we're going to grab our test data which  happens to have a little over four  thousand active policies  next we're going to generate vectors of  future surrender surrender probabilities  under each assumption  third we're going to calculate actual  present values for each policy under  each of our three assumptions  and we're going to use that formula that  i introduced earlier  and lastly we're going to assess  performance and our key question here is  going to be which assumption  yields results  that are closest to the correct  assumption  however before before we can move into  any of these actuarial values though we  need to make a few extra assumptions  that i've listed here i'm not going to  belabor all these um and for our  purposes we can just assume that these  are set in stone however it should be  noted that if this were a real actuarial  model each one of these items would  require its own detailed assumption  setting process as well  okay here's how our model's performed  the total actual present values under  each assumption are  almost indistinguishable from one  another  um all actual to expected ratios are  around 100  and it looks like there's a slight edge  with the random force that's a little  bit closer to the pin  i'm a little bit surprised with these  results with the traditional model  because it looks like it's holding up  pretty well  and i think that's due to some  combination of luck  possibly an indication that the  simulated data here is a little bit too  simplistic and not random enough  and also the fact that  the hyper parameters in our machine  learning models weren't optimized so it  didn't necessarily give them a true a  chance to shine  shifting over to the rmse column the  logistic model really stands out so  let's take a look and see what's going  on with that  and here's what's happening so as i  noted earlier the fit to surrender rates  after year 12 is pretty poor  in addition uh  i'm you can guess that the extrapolation  beyond year 15 is probably driving  towards surrender rates that are  unrealistically low  so what can we do to improve the fit of  this particular model  one approach is that we can apply  judgment like we did before with our  traditional actuarial model  and  here if we look at let's say capping the  policy year variable at 15 and  regenerating surrender predictions  now we can see that the logistic model  looks a lot better  it's still not as close to the random  force to the correct result if you look  at the absolute difference column  uh however the rmse looks much better in  fact it stands out amongst the um  the other assumption bases  uh before we wrap i wanted to show one  last plot comparing the distribution of  residuals between our modeled actuarial  present values and the correct values  a random forest model appears to be  normally distributed with residuals  close to zero  our traditional model  while appearing competitive on the prior  or sorry a logistic model has a mode of  roughly zero but it's clearly skewed and  our traditional model although it  appeared somewhat competitive on the  prior slides it's exhibiting a pretty  wide range of outcomes and the mode  isn't even um  at zero  and uh you know  none of these are ideal observations and  i think a lot of that is due to just  some of the overly simplistic  assumptions that that model's making by  only considering two features  all right so let's wrap up what have we  learned here  modern tools like r and tidy model save  time and unlock deeper insults or  insights resulting in more precise  models  thank you mr freud  assumption model or in assumption  settings should consider should consider  downstream usage in actuarial models  and one last lesson i learned here is  that while algorithms and machine  learning models are really cool they're  not a panacea  expertise uh really matters and  professional judgment is still required  and often unavoidable especially when  you're projecting into the unknown  all right that's it thank you everyone  nerd
 

